1. Spoken letter clips (assets/audio/letters.js)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The eight optional spoken letters (C, H, K, L, Q, R, S, T) were synthesised
with Flite 2.2, the small speech synthesis engine from the Language
Technologies Institute at Carnegie Mellon University (http://cmuflite.org,
https://github.com/festvox/flite; Ubuntu package libflite1 2.2-6build3, run
through ffmpeg's flite source), using the voice cmu_us_slt (US English,
female). That voice was built from the "slt" recordings of the CMU ARCTIC
databases (http://www.festvox.org/cmu_arctic/).

Credit: Spoken letters synthesised with Flite (Language Technologies Institute,
Carnegie Mellon University; voice cmu_us_slt, CMU ARCTIC). Used under the
Flite licence.

Modifications, marked as both licences require: each clip was trimmed of
leading and trailing silence, given one static gain (mean level about
-20 dBFS, peak at most -1 dBFS), faded in over 5 ms and out over 15 ms,
encoded as MP3 (40 kbps, mono, 22.05 kHz) and stored as a base64 data URI.
The method and the script are in docs/dev/audio-notes.md. No Flite code or
voice file ships with Long-Horizon. The names of Carnegie Mellon University
and of the authors are not used to endorse or promote Long-Horizon.

